<div class="wrap page-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">The studio / Common Ground</span><h1>A practice of<br>paying attention.</h1></div><p class="lede">We believe a good building makes everyday life feel a little easier. It holds the light, welcomes change, and belongs to its surroundings.</p></div><div class="wrap studio-layout"><figure class="portrait-photo"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_theme_file_uri( '/assets/images/portfolio/editorial-detail.jpg' ) ); ?>" alt="Weathered beige walls with an arched recess and a tall opening to the sky" width="1600" height="1086" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption class="caption"><span>Architectural reference · illustrative photography</span><span>Annie Spratt / Unsplash</span></figcaption></figure><div class="studio-copy"><span class="eyebrow">Thoughtful by nature</span><h2>Start with what<br>is already good.</h2><p>A well-placed window. A wall with a useful history. A garden that catches the afternoon sun. These are the starting points for our work.</p><p>Common Ground is an imagined independent practice for homeowners and small cultural organizations. Our approach brings the same care to a kitchen threshold as to a shared reading room: understand the daily routines, ask what matters, and give each decision a clear purpose.</p><p>We favor direct conversations, legible drawings, and materials that can be maintained. The ambition is quiet: places that earn their keep, year after year.</p><a class="text-link" href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/services/' ) ); ?>">How we work with you<span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div>
<section class="section wash"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Our principles</span><h2>Three things we return to.</h2><div class="principles"><article><span class="eyebrow">01 / Enough</span><h3>Make space count.</h3><p>Before adding floor area, look at how the existing rooms connect. A simpler route or a shared threshold can unlock more than an extension.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">02 / Endurance</span><h3>Think beyond the finish.</h3><p>Choose for touch, repair, and daily wear. A material should look at home now and remain useful as lives and needs change.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">03 / Belonging</span><h3>Look beyond the boundary.</h3><p>Consider neighbors, streets, and shared edges. Even a modest project can make its little part of the world more welcoming.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class="wrap section"><span class="eyebrow">Working together</span><h2>A clear path, taken together.</h2><ol class="process-list"><li><span class="number">01</span><h3>Listen closely</h3><p>We discuss what works, what gets in the way, and what you can realistically invest. Your brief begins with everyday life.</p></li><li><span class="number">02</span><h3>Explore carefully</h3><p>We compare a small number of meaningful options, explaining the tradeoffs in space, cost, light, and complexity.</p></li><li><span class="number">03</span><h3>Resolve together</h3><p>We develop the preferred direction in stages, with clear decisions and agreed responsibilities before moving forward.</p></li></ol></section><?php get_template_part( 'template-parts/inquiry' ); ?>
